{
  "id": "crashloopbackoff_oomkilled",
  "type": "troubleshooting",
  "question": "What do the resources and livenessProbe fields in the Container schema each define, and what are the sub-schemas they reference?",
  "resource": "io.k8s.api.core.v1.Container",
  "scope": "Namespaced",
  "multi_hop": true,
  "ground_truth": {
    "answer": "The resources field of Container is a ResourceRequirements object containing two maps: requests (used by the scheduler for node placement) and limits (hard caps enforced at runtime). Setting limits.memory prevents a container from using more memory than allowed; exceeding it causes the Linux kernel to OOM-kill the container process. Separately, the livenessProbe field is a Probe object that Kubernetes uses to detect when a running container needs to be restarted. Probe supports three check types (httpGet, tcpSocket, exec) and configurable timing via initialDelaySeconds, periodSeconds, and failureThreshold. Similarly, readinessProbe and startupProbe use the same Probe schema for readiness and startup health checks respectively.",
    "context": [
      "io.k8s.api.core.v1.Container: resources field is of type ResourceRequirements; livenessProbe, readinessProbe, startupProbe are of type Probe",
      "io.k8s.api.core.v1.ResourceRequirements: limits defines maximum compute resources allowed (hard cap); requests defines minimum resources required for scheduling",
      "io.k8s.api.core.v1.Probe: defines a health check; supports httpGet, tcpSocket, exec check types; configurable via initialDelaySeconds, periodSeconds, failureThreshold"
    ],
